      "message": "### Default Arguments on Virtual Functions (Strict Violation)\n\nThe Google C++ Style Guide explicitly dictates: **\"Virtual functions should not have default arguments.\"** This is because default arguments are statically bound at compile time based on the static type of the pointer/reference, not the dynamic type of the object, which can lead to highly confusing behavior if overridden.\n\nIn `usb_interface.h`:\n\n```c\nclass UsbInterface {\n public:\n  static constexpr auto kDefaultTimeout \u003d std::chrono::milliseconds(1000);\n\n  virtual ~UsbInterface() \u003d default;\n\n  virtual std::expected\u003cvoid, UsbError\u003e Connect() \u003d 0;\n\n  virtual std::expected\u003cvoid, UsbError\u003e Send(\n      std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data,\n      std::chrono::milliseconds timeout \u003d kDefaultTimeout) \u003d 0; // Violation\n\n  virtual std::expected\u003cint, UsbError\u003e Receive(\n      std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data,\n      std::chrono::milliseconds timeout \u003d kDefaultTimeout) \u003d 0; // Violation\n};\n\n```\n\n**Remediation:** Remove the default arguments from the `virtual` declarations. If you want the convenience of a default timeout, use an overloaded non-virtual function (potentially in the base class via the Non-Virtual Interface pattern) that calls the virtual function.",
